        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Cycladic culture</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">The islands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyclades">Cyclades</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a>; excavated sites include Chalandriani, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phylakopi">Phylakopi</a>, Skarkos, Saliagos, Kephala and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayia-irini-kea">Ayia Irini</a> on Kea, Grotta on Naxos, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amorgos">Amorgos</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Early Cycladic in three phases, c. 3100–2300 BC; Middle Cycladic from c. 2000 BC; Late Cycladic to c. 1000 BC, when Cycladic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minoan-civilisation">Minoan civilisation</a> had largely converged; the Cycladic and Helladic dating systems are linked in varying ways</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">flat schematic female (rarely male) figurines of white island marble; the Cycladic “frying pans” with pictures of ships; longboats crewed by at least twenty-five oarsmen; pottery traded by sea; early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-working at Kephala; the sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delos">Delos</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavations from the 1880s by antiquaries such as Theodore Bent, then the British School at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Christos Tsountas, who investigated burials on several islands in 1898–99 and coined the term “Cycladic civilization”; pottery sequences and graves; the dating schemes of Oliver Dickinson</span></div>
        </div>
