    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Cycladic culture is best known for flat, schematic marble figurines of uncertain purpose, most of them female.</li><li>Pictures of ships on the clay “frying pans” and Late Cycladic boat models show a seafaring people whose longboats carried crews of at least twenty-five.</li><li>Around 90% of the known figurines may have been looted or removed without record, so their meaning may never be fully understood.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Collectors from the early 1900s prized Cycladic figures for looking like modern sculpture by Jean Arp or Constantin Brâncuși, a demand that fed the looting of graves and a brisk trade in forgeries.</p></aside>
    </section>

